#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "593eeb42-56b8-5177-8a9f-38d08a5186c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12584" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n![](images/e0/e044df6438236be737f2500fb5a96d8f7165822ca3db4b52247007a4a705e20f.svg)\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy=3x(x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/be/be42f33306c634f64bd5830c782a6a8b2cbb376bd124c2ca3edbbd5afc614d9c.svg)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$\n\nSetting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(x-2y)=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y.$ Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3,\n$$\n\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n\n- For $x = 2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124217 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "7c9f4b9e-be60-5da1-a140-08a3f183ce0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12585" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y) \n%= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n![](images/96/96b56307cdc944bde53e3c4e1eba075cc16bac736d834755d681afd001ba83ed.svg)\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,-1) > 0$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) <    0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ![](images/76/764640d69311bf314da0b3dd729f5ac23ada3f536d7a175bfef77435fefad348.svg) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "e5398577-d70c-56f9-9c89-4a9d4e8f1d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-12586" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) \ngiven that\n![](images/00/00e59aa73fc8caed9d9e06876e9352bcbb9123508c1004308ac9593ded669a36.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of 