49dc9fecca67a04f025b89d015a8cc.svg) we obtain\n![](images/d2/d2d5dd3f69b5f511e48cad601f49698f8c6f92aa2c98196b61cc3bdcb9e7419f.svg)\nComputing the determinant ![](images/f5/f5c755c47f2a30ae0db319eaf4b5cff6e7280eaade51fef46f4c20aa956d1f6f.svg) we get\n![](images/ee/eee7490a165a97b1ba1a58445cf275a84053d49790e0207cc2c184f74f1b6d3e.svg)\nNow, since ![](images/01/01e2eff1b6607d38b802f0ecfbc86c7a1d7254094270984c88127af751354c97.svg) and  ![](images/9e/9ecaac33ed65181d517c6a3700f9d07067e4e78b8302a4374697432057e47852.svg) the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) has a local minimum at ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at ![](images/41/41378cd8f4c141c0db7051b606fc860a0c333837e78305a593ce2f5fbd303a32.svg) we obtain\n![](images/84/84190825d65c6d799de0f606c5d0614692d9ec22ba1dc6916a2e6ce6989760d9.svg)\nComputing the determinant ![](images/72/7265b7327f1bfe1fbfb02e958ff01a35790adbc5e1f0984618c97e85e812fca6.svg) we get\n![](images/f0/f0f000997b9e965a8280f51619abfc3c9f6574fbaa6292f045c7d03720693bd9.svg)\nTherefore, ![](images/52/52c679e6f9a0ed40ecf5652d769a673a1cf842a1816d913ddc4e6a3356dc43af.svg) is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) has a local minimum at ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) and a saddle point at ![](images/89/897570ce172a9f169127c297c355bc7a446b1df6ee78afe238f5b53dde75a85c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cbcaa0-0a4b-5073-ad88-c02a4ba1a019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451299} {:answer/id #uuid "375e98ca-c238-53ff-beba-897b64cc63a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cbcaa0-0a4b-5073-ad88-c02a4ba1a019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451299} :answer-field/id #uuid "652213cb-11aa-5dea-a4bc-3b9c8b1c355c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452497} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0d48c5-2f2c-5055-908e-0dc95f5f7282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451301} {:answer/id #uuid "61b6f0c7-25e4-5be3-b321-0d0f90827d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61b6f0c7-25e4-5be3-b321-0d0f90827d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451302} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71cb01d-fa4d-51c3-8af9-10186ba20c84" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd7d602-b04e-508c-8f43-638a3bb96a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451303} {:answer/id #uuid "cafd6976-b47f-5881-a12d-13755ea64993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cafd6976-b47f-5881-a12d-13755ea64993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a57395c-fb2f-5e27-af13-56da5d453d17" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2adfa73-7e18-5d5d-8163-694e5efa8a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-104980" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ has Hessian matrix\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2(y-3) & 2(x+1) \\\\ 2(x+1) & (y-3)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,3)<0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)>0$\n- **III.** The second partial derivative test is inconclusive" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ then:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$;\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the Hessian at $(-1,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(-1,3)=\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{2}+2(3-3) & 2(-1+1) \\\\ 2(-1+1) & (3-3)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The Hessian determinant is\n\n$$\nH(-1,3)=det({∇}^{2}f(-1,3))=1(0)-0(0)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true because\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $H(-1,3)=0,$ so the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61346844-f5db-5d81-988d-5f22f99364e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447690} {:answer/id #uuid "e325de96-c1e7-580b-b09d-da93b3fe3b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e325de96-c1e7-580b-b09d-da93b3fe3b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447691} :answer-field/id #uuid "59cd2a41-7643-579c-a7be-c9328567102c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65db9d78-e369-5af7-bf80-9aaea2cb0ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447692} {:answer/id #uuid "6608fb9a-406b-57eb-94b3-59aa661b60b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65db9d78-e369-5af7-bf80-9aaea2cb0ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447692} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e6461c-403e-5a30-8b5c-3a1d65889782" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9a29471-bdb1-5c51-8ffd-2502d26abe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447694} {:answer/id #uuid "5344e453-2eb8-5606-91f5-05f0b3a8e966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5344e453-2eb8-5606-91f5-05f0b3a8e966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18799fe-c555-58ca-86dc-b96e74bdbd5a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc06ba1-1065-5981-a107-14dc46cea80e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104984" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(0,0)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e870df35-a29a-5740-b1a9-fc27a59659f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364690} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc15fc6-ed64-52d5-ac6c-18f4a5d6f76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364691} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d211e6-d042-5039-a26f-2a692ad7c3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364692} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc138d-043f-51f3-9bd9-aad344676fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364693} {:answer/id #uuid "1188a71b-6d30-5d7c-b671-eeb3e30204cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5d211e6-d042-5039-a26f-2a692ad7c3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a892e43b-67a8-5cd6-aed3-b41a3dd2b871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "265e39ea-f8c9-5146-9ac4-7988205838c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105284" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4=0 \\\\ 6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-4)=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,0)=det({f}^{″}(2,0))=2\\cdot 6-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,0)=({f}^{″}(2,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f584a9-6d95-5612-8744-d5fbfa708ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448602} {:answer/id #uuid "3b704d1a-d453-5ae9-b16e-9648b4cae61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b704d1a-d453-5ae9-b16e-9648b4cae61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4d092d-ab68-55b8-a9dd-d09047e18457" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3f477b4-0a76-545d-9534-9ee9e479b83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448604} {:answer/id #uuid "56bbbe13-48ff-50c2-9e7d-e8f5328a7cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56bbbe13-48ff-50c2-9e7d-e8f5328a7cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448605} :answer-field/id #uuid "891512e6-efc1-5012-886f-462562b19dd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba6c5ce0-1e8c-5b62-a69a-0ab64de4355b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448606} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ea928d-6f64-5b3b-b346-42aee05449b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba6c5ce0-1e8c-5b62-a69a-0ab64de4355b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448606} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c4e907-d6c9-5f4a-bca0-10ea7c035feb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a07adee-cd5f-5906-86f1-fee57d6ba6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37378" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x} & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)=({f}^{″}(1,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0,$ the point $(1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76120093-e343-5052-a462-9eeed4935b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450594} {:answer/id #uuid "555c0670-0865-5a00-b027-f58a0b26c69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "555c0670-0865-5a00-b027-f58a0b26c69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3298d537-d11e-5eb5-b471-be1eb348f6fe" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e466c1b-7f85-5787-9f33-f387b0440aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450596} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b7b99-7dac-506f-8f5e-60849b213bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e466c1b-7f85-5787-9f33-f387b0440aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450596} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c78cee0-1b49-5dcb-820f-d25ea871fcfa" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d90c823-2374-59b3-b675-22903d76217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450598} {:answer/id #uuid "566b5d8d-7821-54bd-ac4b-b928d840baa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d90c823-2374-59b3-b675-22903d76217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450598} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44d6730-238d-5968-83bf-bfd50fc8649d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14dd51d6-4da8-56bc-8036-6e67390713d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37449" :question/problem "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(4,8)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99dcd31-0696-5ae3-a002-0700a699c3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453886} {:answer/id #uuid "b13cab50-ce9f-50ad-b0dc-b0f33b90172e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13cab50-ce9f-50ad-b0dc-b0f33b90172e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453887} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4b6b9c-5d2a-594c-960e-1c4e75c64792" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df70a2f-70bb-5fcc-ae8d-49c71465bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453888} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb49d3c-662c-5e17-871f-5d0e954249ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb49d3c-662c-5e17-871f-5d0e954249ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453889} :answer-field/id #uuid "abbc8d9a-f48f-5dd7-ab15-6b078f09b474" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f80279-e5d1-50e6-9fc4-c16efd13157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453890} {:answer/id #uuid "b82e04f0-14eb-531c-b7af-055ded9e9fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f80279-e5d1-50e6-9fc4-c16efd13157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453890} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdec8465-2901-52b6-a16f-9134ee75d3b0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b007310c-a11f-531c-b695-1febe8ef0462" :question/math-academy-id "q-37589" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2a4e53-1b67-5b4b-b1ff-24dbbe84d43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434952} {:answer/id #uuid "ac84a034-8408-51a1-b95f-a12af62e4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434963} {:answer/id #uuid "af3044d7-995b-5b31-911a-1432e5fb9524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434964} {:answer/id #uuid "ef89c4a6-451f-5b35-895b-c5f4bf15a46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434973} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c1486d-d897-5524-a326-f95ed8d30db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2a4e53-1b67-5b4b-b1ff-24dbbe84d43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434952} :answer-field/id #uuid "4639d8d2-4eac-541e-8594-82bea3d94f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e309db36-c1df-54f7-9f6d-2cd23acc2d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80605" :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8732e6c-4d18-5b7e-ae95-e1ee81edf795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,0)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366374} {:answer/id #uuid "d80b2441-9615-52b5-9198-59bcb8ab0335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366375} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ffbf0-37ac-5f34-8fd8-393118213c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366376} {:answer/id #uuid "73b12864-fe9a-5b7f-9384-e568eee33a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366377} {:answer/id #uuid "df913330-c7ab-564e-8548-6b93fa1857a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df913330-c7ab-564e-8548-6b93fa1857a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366378} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0661b48-e21e-53e3-ab77-d168dba34a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16f8ddbb-3563-5450-8b85-07ba89da4f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-81041" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(0,1))=4\\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\nNow, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(-1,0))=4\\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c2b295-c40e-5849-bc0a-6ab90895eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348468} {:answer/id #uuid "9b87623d-950c-5a5a-bf68-191eabe7390b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349832} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ff7f0e-06dd-5409-86c5-c1a27b7ccac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349890} {:answer/id #uuid "c882bd9a-b5e1-53bc-ab01-c27aef9ae89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350210} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bdfc1-0e7e-55ea-b183-dde5021e752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c882bd9a-b5e1-53bc-ab01-c27aef9ae89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350210} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d87167-b609-5f30-9204-abd258af2b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d35a3af-0061-562b-9545-45ddd0993045" :question/math-academy-id "q-81043" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b98d01b-612c-5ed2-ab6c-3b61169ae757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddcfc4f-27c5-55c4-ba9c-c1904e0e2050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349309} {:answer/id #uuid "7f92b2d4-abd6-5a84-8fd0-d31b55f917b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349619} {:answer/id #uuid "aca6c54b-336c-50d0-ab3a-ecba1f3352d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349977} {:answer/id #uuid "d43d1028-d984-5010-bd02-d37d3bcc5bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43d1028-d984-5010-bd02-d37d3bcc5bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350309} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a680eee-1a4f-5a68-8eb3-d37da5296409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee951228-2bde-5532-9c92-cd8f867b2b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81167" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"}]}
